window.TEASER_LINES = {"versions":[{"id":"deadpan","title":"Deadpan","words":"Agile. Now with fewer humans. You write an idea on a card. A team of AI agents builds it. Another agent tests it. Then it comes back to you, finished, while you were making tea. That is the whole process. There is a Lead Scrum Master. It is not a person. It runs the stand-up anyway, and nobody is late. There is a night watch, which works through the backlog while you sleep, and does not ask for overtime. At 4:20 an alarm goes off. We have been asked not to explain why. The board speaks 120 languages, including pirate and Klingon, which compliance has approved with visible reluctance. If you are worried about cost, there is No Token Mode, in which the agents stop spending and quietly think about their choices. You can send direct messages to friends, who may also be ignoring their backlogs. There is a public live board, so anyone can watch your project being built in real time, like a very slow sport. Every card is tested before you see it. Every test is written down. Every agent knows its job. Nobody has said 'synergy' here, because there are no meetings. Thank you for scrolling. This concludes the pitch."},{"id":"nature","title":"Nature documentary","words":"Behold. The kanban board, at dawn. Here, in its natural habitat, the owner lays a single idea on the board. Within moments, the agents arrive. One builds. One tests. One, the Lead Scrum Master, simply watches, judging everyone quietly. This is how the herd survives. The idea moves across the columns, from To Do, to Doing, to Done, like a wildebeest crossing a very organised river. Few ideas make it. Those that do are tested thoroughly and handed back to the owner, who blinks, and makes another cup of tea. As night falls, the night watch emerges. These nocturnal agents work the backlog in the dark, unseen, while the owner sleeps. At precisely 4:20 a strange alarm sounds across the plains. Scientists still do not know why. The colony communicates in 120 languages, including pirate and Klingon, and on lean days enters No Token Mode, a kind of hibernation to conserve its resources. Some owners send direct messages to friends on neighbouring boards. Others leave their board public, so curious onlookers may watch the whole migration live. It is, truly, one of nature's most efficient spectacles. And yet, somehow, the owner still has forty-seven ideas waiting. Nature is remarkable. Nature is bloody busy."},{"id":"pirate","title":"Pirate captain's log","words":"Arr. Captain's log, day one. I wrote an idea on a card and nailed it to the mast. By sundown me crew of AI deckhands had built it. Not a single mutiny. One builds the thing. One tests the thing. One hands it back, polished like a doubloon. The Lead Scrum Master stands at the wheel and holds a stand-up every morning, which is hard on a ship, as everyone is already standing. When I sleep, the night watch keeps working the backlog by lantern light. At 4:20 a bell rings. The crew will not tell me why, and I have stopped asking. The board speaks 120 tongues, including pirate, obviously, and Klingon, for when we board the wrong ship. When the treasure runs low, we sail in No Token Mode, rowing quietly to save gold. I can send messages to friendly captains on other boards, and our board flies a public flag, so any passing landlubber can watch the work live from shore. I have raised eighty ideas this voyage. Seventy-nine came back finished and tested. The eightieth was 'more rum'. It is in review. Best crew I ever had. They do not even eat the biscuits. Arr."},{"id":"founder","title":"Overexcited founder","words":"Disruption. Okay, so, imagine kanban, but the team is all AI. You just raise an idea. Boom. Agents build it. Agents test it. Agents hand it back. You literally just drink a flat white. We have a Lead Scrum Master who runs stand-ups. It's an AI. It has never once been late. We have a night watch, which is basically your team working the backlog while you sleep, which is the dream, honestly, which is also your dream, because you're asleep. There's an alarm at 4:20. Don't ask. It's culture. We're in 120 languages, including pirate and Klingon, so we are basically already global, and arguably interplanetary. We've got No Token Mode, for when you want to burn zero runway. We've got DMs with your friends. We've got a public live board, so investors can literally watch the product get built, in real time, which, for the record, they have asked us to stop sending them. Every card gets built, tested and handed back with notes. No stand-ups that overrun. No meetings about meetings. No Kevin. We don't have a Kevin. This is the future of work. This is the future of the future. Seed round's open. Please. My mum's asking questions."},{"id":"bedtime","title":"Bedtime story","words":"Once. Once upon a time, there was a board. On the board lived a little card with a big idea. The owner, whose name was Rich, had written it there just before bed. 'Goodnight, little idea,' he said. And he went to sleep. But the board did not sleep. The kind AI agents woke up, one by one. The builder built the idea, very carefully. The tester tested it, twice, to be sure. The Lead Scrum Master held a tiny stand-up, and everybody listened, even the sleepy ones. All through the night, the night watch tiptoed through the backlog, finishing card after card. At 4:20, a little alarm went ding. Nobody knew why. The board knew 120 languages, and it said goodnight in every one of them, even pirate, even Klingon, which sounded a bit like a cough. When the tokens ran low, the board whispered, 'No Token Mode,' and everyone kept very quiet to save them. Friends sent messages from far away boards. Strangers peeked in through the public live board, like looking in a lit window. In the morning, Rich woke up, and there was the idea, built and tested and waiting. The end. Now go to sleep. The board's got it."},{"id":"conspiracy","title":"Conspiracy theory","words":"Wake up. They don't want you to know this. There's a board. You write an idea on it, and a team of AI agents builds it. Coincidence? Then another agent tests it. And then, and this is where it gets weird, it's handed back to you finished. Think about it. Who's running it? A Lead Scrum Master. Nobody's ever seen its face. It holds stand-ups. Every single day. Why? There's a night watch working through the backlog while you sleep. While you sleep. And at 4:20, every night, an alarm goes off. They say it's 'just a feature'. Sure it is. The board speaks 120 languages. Including pirate. Including Klingon. Ask yourself who needs Klingon. There's something called No Token Mode, where it costs nothing. Nothing. Who pays for that? You can DM your friends, supposedly. And there's a public live board, where anyone can watch the work happen, out in the open, which is exactly what they'd do if they had nothing to hide. Which is suspicious. Every card built. Every card tested. Every card handed back. No delays. No excuses. I've raised fifty ideas. They all came back done. I've checked the tea. Open your eyes. Open your board."}],"quips":{"1":"One word. Bold. Minimalist. Investors love it.","10":"Ten words in and it already has more process than your last job.","25":"Twenty-five words. This is roughly where a human team would book a meeting about it.","50":"Fifty. The agents have built three cards while you were dragging that.","75":"Seventy-five words. The Lead Scrum Master would like a quick word. Just one.","100":"One hundred! Halfway. Have a biscuit. The night watch won't tell.","125":"125 words. This pitch now has a backlog of its own.","150":"150. Somewhere, a 4:20 alarm is going off. Nobody knows why.","175":"175 words. We've translated this into Klingon. It's shorter and angrier.","199":"199. One more. Steady. Steady...","200":"Two hundred words. Built, tested and handed back. Done column, here we come.","201":"You've gone too far. There are no more words. The agents have gone to bed. Put the slider down and back away slowly."}};
